Write the newline that an unterminated line still owes before '='.
When a file other than the last ends without a newline, sed holds back
the newline after its last line and writes it before the next output.
The '=' command printed with fprintf, bypassing that, so the number was
glued to the end of the line and the newline came after the number
instead: "sed = f1 f2", with f1 holding "a" and no newline, printed
"1\na2\n\nb\n" rather than "1\na\n2\nb\n". Print the number through
puts_maybe_newline like every other output command, which also checks
for a write error.

---
 editors/sed.c |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/editors/sed.c
+++ b/editors/sed.c
@@ -1270,7 +1270,7 @@
 
 		/* Print line number */
 		case '=':
-			fprintf(G.nonstdout, "%d\n", linenum);
+			sed_puts(itoa(linenum), '\n');
 			break;
 
 		/* Write the current pattern space up to the first newline */
